---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

const headline = 'How to Connect a Custom Domain to Your Website';
const description =
	'Point a domain at a host without breaking email: which DNS records to create, apex versus www, nameservers versus records, propagation, and the six failures that explain almost every “domain not working” ticket.';

const faq = [
	{
		q: 'How long does DNS propagation take?',
		a: 'Usually minutes, sometimes hours, occasionally up to a day or two. The delay is not the host — it is cached records held by resolvers, governed by the TTL you set. Lowering TTL to a few minutes before a planned change shortens the window.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Should I use www or the bare domain?',
		a: 'Pick one and redirect the other permanently. Both are defensible: www is easier to point at a CDN because it can be a CNAME, while the bare domain is shorter to say. What matters is that only one hostname serves the site and the other 301s to it.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Will changing DNS break my email?',
		a: 'It can, and this is the most common way people lose a day. If you switch nameservers, every record — including MX and TXT for mail and domain verification — has to exist at the new provider first. If you only add an A or CNAME record for the website, email is untouched.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Why does my domain show the host’s parking page?',
		a: 'Either the registrar’s default records are still in place, or the host has not been told the domain belongs to the site. Check the A record at the apex and confirm the domain is added in the host’s dashboard; most hosts only serve a site once the domain is registered on their side.',
	},
];

const related = [
	{ href: '/guides/how-to-choose-a-domain-name/', label: 'How to Choose a Domain Name' },
	{ href: '/guides/web-hosting-explained/', label: 'Web Hosting Explained: How to Choose Hosting' },
	{ href: '/guides/ssl-https-explained/', label: 'SSL and HTTPS Explained' },
	{ href: '/guides/website-launch-checklist/', label: 'Website Launch Checklist' },
	{ href: '/guides/carrd-one-page-website/', label: 'How to Build a One-Page Website With Carrd' },
];
---

<GuideLayout
	title={`${headline} | ToolStep`}
	description={description}
	headline={headline}
	canonicalPath="/guides/connect-custom-domain/"
	published="2026-11-10"
	lead="Connecting a domain is two changes that must agree: records at the DNS provider, and the domain registered at the host. Get either one wrong and the site resolves to a parking page, a 404, or nothing at all."
	faq={faq}
	related={related}
	toc={[
		{ href: '#meaning', label: 'What “connecting” means' },
		{ href: '#two-ways', label: 'Two ways to do it' },
		{ href: '#steps', label: 'Step by step' },
		{ href: '#apex', label: 'Apex or www — pick one' },
		{ href: '#https', label: 'HTTPS after DNS' },
		{ href: '#troubleshoot', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
	]}
>
	<div slot="answer" class="answer-box">
		<h2>The short answer</h2>
		<p>
			Add an <strong>A record</strong> at the apex pointing to your host’s IP, and a <strong>CNAME</strong>
			at <code>www</code> pointing to the host’s hostname — or change the domain’s nameservers to the host
			and let it manage everything. Then add the domain in the host dashboard, wait for propagation, and
			enable HTTPS. Redirect one hostname to the other so only one version serves the site.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2 id="meaning">What “connecting” means</h2>
	<p>
		DNS is a set of instructions that tell the internet where your domain’s traffic should go. Three record
		types do nearly all the work for a website:
	</p>
	<div class="table-wrap">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Record</th><th>Maps</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>Hostname → IPv4 address</td><td>The bare domain, usually</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>AAAA</code></td><td>Hostname → IPv6 address</td><td>Optional, and increasingly default</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>CNAME</code></td><td>Hostname → another hostname</td><td><code>www</code>, and CDN endpoints</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>TXT</code></td><td>Hostname → arbitrary text</td><td>Domain verification, SPF, DKIM</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>MX</code></td><td>Domain → mail server</td><td>Email — do not touch unless you mean to</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		A <code>CNAME</code> cannot normally sit at the bare domain, because DNS does not allow a name to have
		both a CNAME and the other records the apex requires. That is why apex records are almost always
		<code>A</code>/<code>AAAA</code>, or a provider-specific “flattened” CNAME.
	</p>

	<h2 id="two-ways">Two ways to do it</h2>
	<div class="table-wrap">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Approach</th><th>What you change</th><th>When to use it</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td><strong>Individual records</strong></td>
					<td>Add or edit A / CNAME at your current DNS provider</td>
					<td>You want to keep DNS (and email) where it is</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td><strong>Nameservers</strong></td>
					<td>Point the domain’s NS records at the host</td>
					<td>The host manages DNS, CDN, and certificates as a package</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		Changing nameservers moves <em>everything</em>, including mail. If your email lives with your current
		DNS provider, either recreate every record at the new one first or stay with individual records. This
		is the single most common way a website migration takes down email for a day.
	</p>

	<h2 id="steps">Step by step</h2>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>Get the exact values from the host.</strong> Copy the A record IP or CNAME target from the host’s domain panel. Do not reuse values from a tutorial — they differ per host and per site.</li>
		<li><strong>Add the domain at the host first.</strong> Most platforms only serve a site after the domain is listed in their dashboard. Doing DNS first produces the “parking page” symptom.</li>
		<li><strong>Create the apex record.</strong> An <code>A</code> record on <code>@</code> (or the bare domain) pointing to the host’s IP.</li>
		<li><strong>Create the www record.</strong> A <code>CNAME</code> on <code>www</code> pointing to the host’s target hostname, if the host asks for one.</li>
		<li><strong>Add verification records if requested.</strong> A <code>TXT</code> record proves ownership for the host or for a search console property.</li>
		<li><strong>Remove conflicting records.</strong> Old parking A records, duplicate A records pointing elsewhere, or a stale CNAME will fight the new setup.</li>
		<li><strong>Wait, then verify.</strong> Check the resolved record and the HTTP response — see the checks below.</li>
	</ol>
	<p>
		If you use Cloudflare in front of the host, set the record to “DNS only” during setup. A proxied
		(orange-cloud) record hides the origin and will mask the misconfiguration you are trying to diagnose.
	</p>

	<h2 id="apex">Apex or www — pick one</h2>
	<p>
		Both work; only one should serve the site. Decide, then redirect the other with a permanent 301. This
		matters beyond tidiness: two hostnames serving identical content create duplicate URLs, split links,
		and confuse canonical signals. Our
		<a href="/guides/seo-checklist-new-website/">SEO checklist</a> treats hostname consolidation as a
		launch blocker for that reason.
	</p>
	<p>
		If you expect to use a CDN or a platform that hands you a target hostname, <code>www</code> is the
		easier primary because it can be a CNAME. If the domain is short and spoken aloud often, the apex is
		prettier. Either is fine; inconsistency is not.
	</p>

	<h2 id="https">HTTPS after DNS</h2>
	<p>
		Certificates are issued to hostnames, so they can only be issued once DNS resolves to the host. The
		sequence is: DNS first, then certificate. Most hosts automate this and issue within minutes of the
		domain resolving; some require you to press a button. If issuance fails, the cause is almost always
		one of three things: the domain does not resolve yet, a second A record points elsewhere, or a
		<code>CAA</code> record blocks the certificate authority.
	</p>
	<p>
		The details of certificate types, mixed content, and HSTS are in
		<a href="/guides/ssl-https-explained/">SSL and HTTPS explained</a>.
	</p>

	<h2 id="troubleshoot">Troubleshooting</h2>
	<div class="table-wrap">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Most likely cause</th><th>Check</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Host’s parking page</td><td>Domain not added at the host, or registrar default records still active</td><td>Dashboard domain list, then the apex A record</td></tr>
				<tr><td>“Site can’t be reached”</td><td>Wrong IP, or the record has not propagated</td><td>Resolve the A record from a different network</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Works without www, fails with it</td><td>Missing or wrong CNAME on <code>www</code></td><td>The <code>www</code> record value against the host’s instructions</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Certificate error</td><td>Issued before DNS settled, or CAA blocking</td><td>Re-issue after DNS resolves; check CAA</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Email stopped</td><td>Nameservers changed without recreating MX/TXT</td><td>MX records at the new DNS provider</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Intermittent behaviour</td><td>Two A records pointing at different servers</td><td>Full record list, not just the first entry</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		Verify with two independent checks: resolve the record itself, then request the page and look at the
		status code and the final URL. A record that looks correct but serves a 404 means the host is not
		configured for that hostname yet.
	</p>
</GuideLayout>
